.TH HD-KERNEL-SERVER 7 "November 2026" "hd-kernel-server" "HyprDebian"
.SH NAME
hd-kernel-server \- the HyprDebian kernel for servers
.SH DESCRIPTION
The package
.B hd-kernel-server
depends on the most recent HyprDebian kernel for servers of the suite and its
ZFS module, so that they are installed and upgraded with it:
.TP
.BI linux-image- release
The kernel of
.BR hd-kernel (7):
the kernel of Debian, with the patches and the config of Debian, built by the
build server of HyprDebian, but with only the modules that a server needs.
.TP
.BI zfs-modules- release
The ZFS module of the
.B zfs-linux
of Debian, built against that kernel, in
.IR /lib/modules/ release /extra/zfs .
It provides
.BR zfs-modules ,
which
.B zfs-initramfs
takes in place of
.BR zfs-dkms .
.PP
What the config of Debian builds into the kernel stays, and so do the modules
that are not drivers: the file systems, the network protocols and netfilter,
crypto, and kvm, for virtual machines; not the protocols of wireless,
Bluetooth and other hardware that a server has not, and not RDMA and
InfiniBand. Of the drivers only these stay:
.IP \(bu 2
those of the hardware of servers: SATA and NVMe disks; the SAS and RAID cards
of Intel, Broadcom, Adaptec and HPE, with their enclosures; Fibre Channel of
QLogic; the network cards of Intel, Broadcom, Realtek, Mellanox ConnectX and
Brocade, with Fibre Channel; IPMI, the memory errors, temperatures, watchdog
and SMBus of the Intel chipset; the graphics of the BMC; the platform drivers
of Dell; USB with keyboards, mice and storage;
.IP \(bu 2
those of storage targets: the SCSI target with iSCSI, Fibre Channel over
Ethernet, loopback and the user space backends of tcmu, Fibre Channel with the
target mode of QLogic cards, and NVMe over TCP, Fibre Channel and loopback;
.IP \(bu 2
those of software: loop, zram and nbd, device mapper and RAID, and the network
devices of containers, virtual machines, VPNs and bonds, such as tun, veth,
macvlan, vxlan, wireguard and vhost.
.PP
The release is
.IB x.y.z +debNN-hdserver id -amd64\fR,
such as
.BR 7.2.12+deb14-hdserver1a2b3c-amd64 ,
where
.I id
comes from the versions of
.BR linux ,
.B zfs-linux
and
.BR hd-kernel-server .
A new build has a new release, so it installs next to the kernel that runs.
.PP
Every build signs the modules of the kernel and the ZFS module with a key it
makes for the build and then throws away; the kernel trusts that key and no
other for its modules.
.SH SWITCHING
To switch a server to the HyprDebian kernel for servers:
.PP
.RS
sudo apt install hd-kernel-server
.RE
.PP
This removes the other HyprDebian kernels,
.BR hd-kernel ,
.B hd-kernel-vm
and
.BR hd-kernel-desktop ,
and
.BR linux-image-amd64 ,
.B linux-headers-amd64
and
.BR linux-base-amd64 ,
so the other kernels get no more upgrades.
After the installation the bootloader shows the new kernel at the top of its
menu, with
.B -hd
in its name, see
.BR hd-bootloader-update (8).
Before the server boots it, check that the kernel has the drivers of its
disks and network cards: a driver that is not in
.IR /lib/modules/ release
is not in the kernel.
Once it boots, the other kernels can be purged, as
.BR hd-kernel (7)
describes for the kernels of Debian.
.PP
To switch back to the kernel of
.BR hd-kernel ,
or to that of Debian:
.PP
.RS
sudo apt install hd-kernel
.br
sudo apt install linux-image-amd64 linux-headers-amd64
.RE
.PP
Either removes
.BR hd-kernel-server ;
its kernels stay until they are purged.
.SH FILES
.TP
.IR /boot/vmlinuz- release
.TQ
.IR /lib/modules/ release
The kernel and its modules.
.TP
.IR /lib/modules/ release /extra/zfs
The ZFS module.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR hd-bootloader-update (8),
.BR hd-buildserver (8),
.BR hd-kernel (7),
.BR hd-kernel-desktop (7),
.BR hd-kernel-vm (7),
.BR zfs (4)
